# Target C-II - reflection blocker interfaces are inward and terminate at loaded sinks

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the shallow-tooth reflection branch; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** Type-II full-core `eta=3/26` reflection modules after block diagonalization and thin-shell path reduction  
**Dependencies:** simultaneous reflection block diagonalization; thin-shell blocker path theorem; geometric contact tubes; canonical C26 fully force-visible barycenter/degree theorem; relative-interior full actual diameter support  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_radial_interface_loaded_sinks_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The thin-shell theorem left one explicit local target: classify unit-contact edges from

\[
U=\{z\in A:|z-q|>D\}
\]

to the inner annulus. The interface is one-sided. Every contact leaving `U` points strictly toward `q`, every such edge lands in

\[
D-1<|v-q|\le D,
\]

and one blocker vertex has at most three contacts leaving `U`.

There is a stronger force consequence. Canonical C26 says that a nonloaded contact vertex is a strict positive contact barycenter and has full contact degree `3`, `4`, or `5`. Applied to the radial function `R(v)=|v-q|`, this forces every nonloaded, nonisolated blocker to have a contact neighbor still in `U` whose squared radius is larger by more than one. Following such contacts terminates at a positively diameter-loaded blocker.

Together with the proved path structure of `G_C[U]`, this gives

\[
\boxed{
\text{every component of }G_C[U]
\text{ either contains a loaded vertex, or is one contact-isolated vertex.}
}
\tag{RI.1}
\]

Under the relative-interior normalization, the second alternative has no actual diameter edge either. The canonical farthest blocker therefore satisfies

\[
\boxed{y\in B_+\quad\text{or}\quad \deg_C(y)=\deg_H(y)=0.}
\tag{RI.2}
\]

Deep contact buffers also retain the blocker phase. At the current near-quarter depth they can move radially by `D^(1/4-o(1))`, but their additional angular drift is only `O(D^-3/4)` and is negligible beside the original `D^-1/4` module cone.

---

## 1. Exact geometry of one contact leaving `U`

Fix one module, suppress its index, and set

\[
R(v):=|v-q|.
\]

The shell theorem gives

\[
D<R(z)<D+h_*,
\qquad
h_*={\sqrt{303}\over52}=0.3347479843\ldots<1,
\qquad z\in U.
\tag{RI.3}
\]

Take `z in U`, `v notin U`, and `|v-z|=1`. Write

\[
r=R(z)=D+\delta,
\qquad
s=R(v)\le D,
\qquad
0<\delta<h_*,
\]

and

\[
e={z-q\over r},
\qquad
d=v-z,
\qquad |d|=1.
\]

The triangle inequality gives

\[
\boxed{D-1<s\le D.}
\tag{RI.4}
\]

Also

\[
s^2=r^2+1+2r(e\cdot d).
\]

Since `s<=D`,

\[
(-e)\cdot d
\ge {r^2+1-D^2\over2r}.
\]

For `r=D+delta`, the right side is exactly

\[
\boxed{
{r^2+1-D^2\over2r}
=\delta+{1-\delta^2\over2(D+\delta)}
>\delta>0.
}
\tag{RI.5}

Thus every boundary contact points strictly inward toward `q`, and its inward radial component exceeds the blocker's radial excess `R(z)-D`.

The allowed unit directions form an arc centered at `-e` of width

\[
2\arccos\!\left({r^2+1-D^2\over2r}\right)<\pi.
\tag{RI.6}
\]

---

## 2. At most three contacts leave one blocker

Let `v_1,...,v_m` be distinct contact neighbors of one `z in U` outside `U`. All corresponding unit directions lie in the strict sub-`pi` arc (RI.6).

For distinct `i,j`, source separation gives `|v_i-v_j|>=1`. Because both points lie on the unit circle about `z`, their angular separation at `z` is at least `pi/3`.

Four directions ordered inside one arc would therefore have three consecutive gaps with total at least `pi`, impossible in an arc of width strictly below `pi`. Hence

\[
\boxed{|N_C(z)\setminus U|\le3.}
\tag{RI.7}

The previous shell theorem already gives

\[
\Delta(G_C[U])\le2
\]

and makes every component of `G_C[U]` an angularly monotone path or an isolated vertex. Equation (RI.7) classifies the first outward interface left open there.

---

## 3. Deep contact buffers stay in one polar column

Let `v` have contact-graph distance at most an integer `R<D/2` from `U`. Choose `z in U` joined to `v` by a contact path of length at most `R`. Then

\[
|v-z|\le R,
\]

so (RI.3) gives

\[
\boxed{D-R<R(v)<D+h_*+R.}
\tag{RI.8}
\]

For nonzero vectors `a,b`,

\[
\left|{a\over|a|}-{b\over|b|}\right|
\le {2|a-b|\over\min\{|a|,|b|\}}.
\]

Apply this to `a=z-q`, `b=v-q`. If `phi` is their angular difference, then

\[
2\sin{\phi\over2}\le {2R\over D-R},
\]

hence

\[
\boxed{\phi\le2\arcsin{R\over D-R}.}
\tag{RI.9}

The geometric contact-tube theorem already gives, for all sufficiently large `D`,

\[
\angle(z-q,n)\le C_AD^{-1/4},
\qquad
C_A=9.56283745460\ldots .
\tag{RI.10}

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\angle(v-q,n)
\le C_AD^{-1/4}+2\arcsin{R\over D-R}.
}
\tag{RI.11}

At the current near-quarter choice

\[
R_D=(1+o(1)){D^{1/4}\over\omega_D^2},
\qquad
\omega_D\to\infty,
\]

we have

\[
2\arcsin{R_D\over D-R_D}
=O\!\left({D^{-3/4}\over\omega_D^2}\right)
=o(D^{-1/4}).
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
N_{R_D}(U)
\text{ remains inside the same }
(C_A+o(1))D^{-1/4}
\text{ polar cone.}
}
\tag{RI.12}

A long contact obstruction cannot escape by rotating into another phase.

---

## 4. C26 forces radial ascent inside the blocker set

Let `B_+` be the loaded vertex set for the relative-interior normalized C-II stress. Canonical C26 says that if `z notin B_+` has at least one unit contact, then:

1. `deg_C(z)` is `3`, `4`, or `5`; and
2. there are coefficients `lambda_zu>0` with

\[
\sum_{u\sim_C z}\lambda_{zu}(u-z)=0.
\tag{RI.13}
\]

Fix such a `z in U`, put `r=R(z)` and `e=(z-q)/r`. For any contact neighbor `u`, writing `s=R(u)` gives

\[
\boxed{
e\cdot(u-z)={s^2-r^2-1\over2r}.}
\tag{RI.14}

Dot (RI.13) with `e`. Suppose no contact neighbor had positive radial component. Then every term in the resulting scalar sum would be nonpositive, so positivity of every `lambda_zu` would force

\[
e\cdot(u-z)=0
\]

for **every** contact neighbor.

A unit vector orthogonal to `e` has only the two possible directions `+Je` and `-Je`. Hence at most two distinct contact neighbors can have zero radial component. This contradicts the C26 degree lower bound `deg_C(z)>=3`.

Therefore some contact neighbor has positive radial component. By (RI.14), for that neighbor

\[
\boxed{R(u)^2>R(z)^2+1.}
\tag{RI.15}

In particular `R(u)>R(z)>D`, so

\[
\boxed{u\in U.}
\tag{RI.16}

This is the useful force statement: the inner buffer cannot by itself balance a nonloaded blocker. Every nonloaded nonisolated blocker has an outward contact **inside the blocker shell**.

---

## 5. Loaded-sink decomposition of `G_C[U]`

Orient an internal blocker contact `zu` from `z` to `u` whenever

\[
R(u)^2>R(z)^2+1.
\]

By Section 4, every nonloaded, nonisolated blocker has at least one outgoing internal edge. Radius strictly increases along every directed edge, so directed cycles are impossible.

Start at any nonloaded, nonisolated blocker and repeatedly follow an outgoing edge. Finiteness forces termination. The terminal vertex still lies in the same component of `G_C[U]` and has at least one contact. If it were nonloaded, Section 4 would supply another outgoing internal edge. Therefore it is loaded.

Hence every nontrivial component of `G_C[U]` contains a vertex of `B_+`.

Now let `{z}` be a singleton component. If `z` has any contact outside `U` and is nonloaded, Section 4 again forces an internal blocker neighbor, contradiction. Thus a singleton with any contact is loaded. Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\text{each component of }G_C[U]
\text{ contains }B_+,
\text{ or is one full-contact isolate.}
}
\tag{RI.17}

The relative-interior C-II stress has every actual diameter edge in its positive diameter support. Therefore a vertex outside `B_+` cannot be an actual diameter endpoint. The unanchored singleton in (RI.17) satisfies

\[
\boxed{\deg_C(z)=\deg_H(z)=0.}
\tag{RI.18}

It has no old active constraint at either distance endpoint.

There is also an exact but nonclosing ascent-length estimate. Let `y` be farthest from `q`, so the shell theorem gives

\[
R(y)<D+h_*.
\]

Every ascent edge increases squared radius by more than one. Thus a radial-ascent chain beginning anywhere in `U` has length below

\[
\boxed{
(D+h_*)^2-D^2
=2h_*D+h_*^2
<0.670D+1.
}
\tag{RI.19}

This linear bound is weaker than the isolated near-quarter contact depth. Its value is structural: ascent reaches the actual diameter support without leaving `U`.

---

## 6. Canonical farthest head: loaded or completely free

Let `y` be the canonical farthest blocker from the support-normal theorem:

\[
R(y)=\max_{z\in A}R(z)>D.
\]

If `y` has a unit contact and is nonloaded, Section 4 produces a contact neighbor `u in U` with

\[
R(u)^2>R(y)^2+1,
\]

contradicting maximality. Therefore

\[
\boxed{y\in B_+\quad\text{or}\quad\deg_C(y)=0.}
\tag{RI.20}

In the second case `y notin B_+`, and full relative-interior diameter support gives

\[
\boxed{\deg_C(y)=\deg_H(y)=0.}
\tag{RI.21}

Thus a nonloaded canonical head is an exposed full-source hull vertex with no old unit contact and no old diameter pair. There is no third case in which the canonical head is unloaded but trapped inside a contact network.

---

## 7. Near-quarter simultaneous consequence

Take the current diagonal

\[
\omega_D\to\infty,
\qquad
\omega_D=o(D^{1/8}),
\qquad
m_D=\lfloor\omega_D\rfloor,
\]

with mutually disjoint module buffers of depth

\[
r_D=(1-o(1)){D^{1/4}\over\omega_D^2}.
\]

Choose the canonical farthest head `y_i` in every module. The sets `U_i` are pairwise disjoint, so the heads are distinct. Apply (RI.20) and retain the larger class.

Either at least `m_D/2-O(1)` heads are loaded, or at least `m_D/2-O(1)` are unloaded full-contact isolates.

In the loaded case, each selected head is an endpoint of a positive actual diameter edge. One diameter edge has only two endpoints, so the selected heads support at least

\[
\boxed{m_D/4-O(1)=\Omega(\omega_D)}
\tag{RI.22}

**distinct actual diameter edges**, while their reflection modules remain mutually isolated through depth `r_D`.

In the unloaded case, at least

\[
\boxed{m_D/2-O(1)}
\tag{RI.23}

canonical exposed heads satisfy

\[
\deg_C(y_i)=\deg_H(y_i)=0,
\]

again one per mutually isolated reflection module.

For `omega_D=log log D`, this gives an unbounded family of either actual-diameter-anchored canonical heads or completely old-active-constraint-free canonical heads, with module isolation depth

\[
{D^{1/4}\over(\log\log D)^2}(1-o(1)).
\]

---

## 8. Frontier effect

The surviving local reflection obstruction now has this form:

```text
blocker shell U:
    D < |z-q| < D+0.334748
    induced contact graph = angularly monotone paths / isolates

contact leaving U:
    lands in D-1 < |v-q| <= D
    points strictly toward q
    inward radial component > |z-q|-D
    at most three leaving contacts per blocker

deep buffer:
    remains in the same inverse-quarter polar column

nonloaded nonisolated blocker:
    has an internal U-contact raising squared radius by >1
    radial ascent terminates at a loaded blocker

component of G_C[U]:
    contains a positive-diameter-loaded vertex
    OR is one contact- and diameter-isolated source point

canonical farthest head:
    loaded
    OR completely contact- and diameter-isolated
```

This does not close C-II. The loaded-sink branch can terminate directly on the actual diameter system. The free-head branch gives only local old-constraint freedom; it does not yet prove that an inward compensation can travel the finite distance needed to remove all `q-U` violations before a new contact or diameter event appears.

The next useful theorem is correspondingly narrower: either rule out a thin-shell monotone blocker path terminating at a loaded radial peak while preserving its attached actual-diameter geometry, or prove that a FREE-HEAD can be continued through its first newly active event until the whole blocker component is repaired.

**Disposition:** proved the exact inward first interface, three-valent boundary cap, polar persistence of near-quarter contact buffers, and a C26 radial-ascent theorem forcing every nontrivial blocker component onto the positive actual diameter support. The canonical blocker head is now either diameter-loaded or completely contact- and diameter-isolated. C-II and Level I remain open.
